{
  "result": "Every morning, a small bakery opens before the rest of the street wakes. Its windows glow amber, and the air outside smells of butter and yeast. The baker, Mara, likes this hour best, when the world seems briefly willing to start over without asking anyone for an explanation or permission.\n\nOne Tuesday, she finds a folded note beneath the door. It says, \u201cFor the person who needs breakfast but cannot afford it.\u201d Inside is a crisp paper bill. Mara makes a loaf, places it on the counter, and writes a sign: \u201cTake this if you need it. No questions asked.\u201d\n\nBy noon, the loaf is gone. In its place sits another note: \u201cThank you. I will remember.\u201d The next morning, someone leaves two oranges beside the bread. Then a jar of jam appears, followed by a bag of apples and a packet of tea. Nobody knows who brings them all.\n\nThe gifts are not always practical. A child leaves a drawing of a purple dog. An elderly man contributes a poem about rain. Mara tapes both to the window, where they curl gently in the warmth. Customers begin lingering, talking to strangers they once hurried past without a glance.\n\nMonths later, Mara cannot say who left the first note. Perhaps that is why the tradition lasts. It belongs to nobody in particular, so everyone can claim a little part of it. Each morning, the bakery opens, and the street begins again, a little kinder than it had been before.",
